    "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities": {
      "supplier": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
      "status": "draft_unbound",
      "actual_file": "items/def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities.md",
      "actual_status": "draft",
      "actual_pipeline_run": "absent",
      "actual_frontmatter_parseable": true,
      "actual_domain": "physics",
      "actual_proof_provenance": "not-applicable",
      "actual_section": "Definition",
      "actual_statement_excerpt": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. The length action $-mc\\int ds$ has the same units, and $\\tau=s/c$ has seconds. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model's normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. The restriction of $g^{-1}$ to covectors annihilating $n$ is positive definite, so it supplies the norm of $q_n(p)$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
      "actual_dependencies": [
        "def-countable-choice",
        "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
        "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
        "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline"
      ],
