      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On $GL(4,\\mathbb R)$ define $\\Phi(L)=L^T\\eta L$ with values in the ten-dimensional space of symmetric matrices. At $\\Phi(L)=\\eta$, its derivative is $D\\Phi_L(\\Delta)=\\Delta^T\\eta L+L^T\\eta\\Delta$. For any symmetric $S$, set $\\Delta=L\\eta^{-1}S/2$; using $L^T\\eta L=\\eta$ and $S^T=S$, each summand equals $S/2$. Thus $D\\Phi_L$ is onto.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Every point of $\\Phi^{-1}(\\eta)$ is regular by step 1.1, and the level is nonempty because it contains $I$. Thus [F3] makes $O(1,3)=\\Phi^{-1}(\\eta)$ an embedded six-dimensional submanifold of $GL(4,\\mathbb R)$. For $L\\in O(1,3)$, $Le_0$ is timelike and $L$ is a homeomorphism of the timelike set; [F4] implies that $L$ preserves the selected future component exactly when $Le_0$ lies in that component. Since $e_0$ is the positive time-axis vector, this is exactly $L^0{}_0>0$. Also $|L^0{}_0|\\ge1$, and taking determinants in $L^T\\eta L=\\eta$ gives $(\\det L)^2=1$. Therefore $\\det L=1$ and $L^0{}_0>0$ select an open subset of $O(1,3)$. By [F1] this subset is $SO^+(1,3)$. Matrix multiplication and inversion restrict smoothly from $GL(4)$, so this subgroup is a six-dimensional Lie group.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Give $G=\\mathbb R^4\\times SO^+(1,3)$ the product manifold structure from step 2.1 and multiplication $(a,\\Lambda)(b,L)=(a+\\Lambda b,\\Lambda L)$. Both parenthesizations of a triple product equal $(a+\\Lambda b+\\Lambda Lc,\\Lambda LM)$; the identity is $(0,I)$; and direct substitution shows $(-\\Lambda^{-1}a,\\Lambda^{-1})$ is a left and right inverse. Multiplication and inversion are smooth because matrix multiplication and inversion on $SO^+(1,3)$ are smooth and the translation operations are smooth. The affine matrix map $\\rho(a,\\Lambda)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\Lambda&a\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ is injective, has smooth inverse onto its image by block projection, and is a group homomorphism because matrix multiplication gives $\\rho(a+\\Lambda b,\\Lambda L)$. The product manifold tangent at identity is $\\mathbb R^4\\times T_I SO^+(1,3)$. By [F5], the latter tangent space is contained in the six-dimensional space of matrices satisfying $A^T\\eta+\\eta A=0$; both have dimension six by step 2.1 and [F5], so they are equal. Hence $d\\rho_I(a,A)=\\begin{pmatrix}A&a\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$. A group homomorphism carries left-invariant vector fields to related left-invariant fields: differentiating $\\rho\\circ L_g=L_{\\rho(g)}\\circ\\rho$ gives this relation. Related fields' brackets are related, since applying each bracket to a smooth test function and expanding $X(Yf)-Y(Xf)$ gives the same identity after composition with $\\rho$. For matrices $M,N$, the left-invariant fields on $GL(5)$ are $g\\mapsto gM$ and $g\\mapsto gN$; differentiating these fields gives their commutator at the identity as $MN-NM$. Thus the bracket of two pair matrices is $\\begin{pmatrix}[A,B]&Ab-Ba\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$, yielding $[(a,A),(b,B)]=(Ab-Ba,[A,B])$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The cotangent lift preserves the covector-tangent pairing: $p'(dx')=(\\Lambda^{-T}p)(\\Lambda dx)=p(dx)$. For the one-parameter element $\\exp(-s(a,A))$, differentiation of $x'=\\Lambda_s x+a_s$ gives the fundamental field $(-(a+Ax),A^Tp)$ on $(x,p)$; its negative is the positive cotangent-lifted field $(a+Ax,-A^Tp)$. Pairing the covector with the positive base generator gives $\\ell=p_b(a^b+A^b{}_cx^c)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
