        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Define $\\widetilde S(t,a)$ by the integral in the statement. Differentiating with respect to $a$ and integrating the $p\\cdot\\partial_a\\dot q$ term by parts yields $\\partial_a\\widetilde S=dS_0(a)+p(t,a)D_aq(t,a)-p(0,a)D_aq(0,a)+\\int_0^t[-\\dot p-H_q]D_aq+[\\dot q-H_p]D_ap\\,du.$ The initial terms cancel because $p(0,a)=dS_0(a)$ and $D_aq(0,a)=I$; both integrands vanish by Hamilton's equations [L1]. Thus $\\partial_a\\widetilde S=p(t,a)D_aq(t,a)$.",
