            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-coadjoint-orbits-are-symplectic-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $\\mathcal O\\subseteq\\mathfrak g^*$ be a\ncoadjoint orbit with its canonical immersed homogeneous-space structure and let\n$\\omega$ be the KKS form of",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In the rest frame $P=(mc,0)$, the spatial components of $W$ are the three independent duals of $J^{ij}$, multiplied by the nonzero factor $mc$. Thus $|\\mathbf W|=mcs$ and $W^0=0$, so $W\\cdot P=0$ and $W^2=m^2c^2s^2$. Under translations, $J$ changes by $a\\wedge P$; inserting this into $W$ adds an epsilon contraction with two copies of $P$, which vanishes by antisymmetry. Lorentz covariance of $\\varepsilon$ under $SO^+$ and the charge action [L1] preserve these scalar identities over the orbit.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Any future timelike $P$ with $P^2=-m^2c^2$ can be sent to rest by a proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation. In that frame, choose the translation part $a$ to remove the three $J^{0i}$ components; the remaining spatial spin vector has norm $s$ by step 1.1, and a spatial rotation sends it to the specified $J^{12}=s$ axis. Therefore every pair with the stated invariants lies in the rest representative's orbit; the converse follows from step 1.1.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "A stabilizer element must fix $P=(mc,0)$, so its Lorentz part is a spatial rotation. Preserving the nonzero spin vector leaves exactly the $SO(2)$ rotations about that axis, of dimension one. In the coadjoint action, preserving $J^{0i}=0$ then forces the spatial translation part to vanish, while translations parallel to $P$ remain free, giving one further dimension. The stabilizer has dimension two, and since $\\dim G=10$ by [F1], the orbit dimension is eight.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Applying [F2] to this orbit gives its canonical smooth homogeneous-space structure and nondegenerate closed KKS two-form. If $s=0$, the stabilizer instead contains all three spatial rotations and the one translation parallel to $P$, hence has dimension four and its orbit dimension is $10-4=6$. This establishes the stabilizer jump without asserting regularity of any ambient Casimir locus.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "A1"
          ]
        }
      ],
