      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In a rest frame $P=(mc,0)$, $W=0$ is equivalent to $J^{ij}=0$, since its three spatial components are an invertible multiple of the three spatial angular-momentum components. The remaining $J^{0i}$ are produced by a spatial position; equivalently for a transverse representative $x\\cdot P=0$, $x^a=J^{ab}p_b/P^2$, as substitution of $J=x\\wedge P$ gives $J^{ab}p_b=x^aP^2-(x\\cdot P)P^a$. This characterization is Lorentz covariant.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "H8's quotient point $(a,p)$ maps to $P^0=E/c$, $P^i=p_i$, $J^{0i}=-a^iP^0$, and $J^{ij}=a^ip_j-a^jp_i$. The inverse is $p_i=P^i$, $a^i=-J^{0i}/P^0$, smooth since $P^0>0$. These mutually inverse smooth coordinates make the image an embedded six-dimensional graph and show the quotient map is a diffeomorphism onto the charge locus of step 1.1.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "A proper orthochronous boost sends each future massive $P$ to rest. By step 1.1 its $W=0$ charge then has only $J^{0i}$ components, and a translation removes those components. Thus every pair in the locus is in the orbit of $(mc,0;0)$; conversely $P^2$ and $W=0$ are invariant under the coadjoint action. The stabilizer at rest consists of spatial rotations and translations parallel to $P$, dimension $3+1=4$, so the orbit dimension is $10-4=6$. The six smooth orbit coordinates $p_i=P^i$, $a^i=-J^{0i}/P^0$ from step 2.1 have injective differential: if their differentials vanish, the graph formulas in step 2.1 make every component of $(P,J)$ have zero differential, and the coadjoint-orbit inclusion is an immersion. Since both manifolds have dimension six, the inverse function theorem makes this coordinate map a local diffeomorphism. The bijection in step 2.1 is therefore a global diffeomorphism for the canonical orbit smooth structure.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\rho:\\Sigma_m^+\\to B_m$ be the H8 quotient and $\\iota:\\Sigma_m^+\\hookrightarrow T^*\\mathbb R^4$ the inclusion. H8 states $\\rho^*\\omega_{\\rm red}=\\iota^*\\omega$. The moment components from [L1] are constant on the characteristic fibres by step 3.1, and the Poincare action descends, so for any tangent $v$ on $B_m$ choose a lift $\\tilde v$ and obtain $d\\bar\\mu^\\xi(v)=d\\mu^\\xi(\\tilde v)=-\\omega_{\\rm red}(\\xi_{B_m},v)$. On the orbit [F2] gives the same moment equation. Its fundamental fields span each tangent space, so these equations determine both two-forms pointwise and show the diffeomorphism in step 3.1 pulls KKS back to $\\omega_{\\rm red}=da^i\\wedge dp_i$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
