      "unresolved_inputs": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-rpm-h-almost-regular-legendre",
          "status": "draft_unbound",
          "actual_file": "items/def-rpm-h-almost-regular-legendre.md",
          "actual_status": "draft",
          "actual_pipeline_run": "absent",
          "actual_frontmatter_parseable": true,
          "actual_domain": "mathematics",
          "actual_proof_provenance": "not-applicable",
          "actual_section": "Definition",
          "actual_statement_excerpt": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $\\mathcal D$ be an open subset of the time-dependent tangent bundle $\\mathbb R\\times TQ$ of a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $Q$, and let $L:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For fixed $t$, the **fibre derivative** is the map\n\n$$\\mathbb F L(t,q,v)=(t,q,p),\\qquad p(w)=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}L(t,q,v+sw),$$\n\nas in [[def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian]]. In induced coordinates $p_i=\\partial L/\\partial v^i$.\n\nThe Lagrangian is **regular** at $(t,q,v)$ when the velocity Hessian $(\\partial^2L/\\partial v^i\\partial v^j)$ is invertible there; regularity gives only a local inverse branch. It is **hyperregular on a declared domain** when $\\mathbb F L$ is a diffeomorphism from that entire domain onto an open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$.\n\nIt is **almost regular on a declared domain $\\mathcal D_0\\subseteq\\mathcal D$** when the following are all assumed: $\\mathbb F L|_{\\mathcal D_0}$ has constant rank; its image $N$ is an embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$; its corestriction $\\mathcal D_0\\to N$ is a surjective submersion (its differential is surjective at every point); and every fibre of that corestriction is connected. These hypotheses are not consequences of singularity alone.",
          "actual_dependencies": [
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian",
