      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For fixed $(t,\\alpha)$ the map $q\\mapsto S_\\alpha(t,q,\\alpha)$ has derivative $S_{\\alpha q}=(S_{q\\alpha})^T$, invertible by hypothesis. [F1] gives a local smooth solution $q=q(t,\\alpha,\\beta)$ of $\\beta=S_\\alpha$. Set $p=S_q$. Then $d_qp=S_{qq}dq+S_{q\\alpha}d\\alpha$ and $d_q\\beta=S_{\\alpha q}dq+S_{\\alpha\\alpha}d\\alpha$. Symmetry of second derivatives gives $dq\\wedge dp=d\\beta\\wedge d\\alpha$, so the transformation is canonical.",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Along a characteristic, $\\dot q=H_p$ and $\\dot\\alpha=0$. Differentiate the HJ equation with respect to $\\alpha$: $S_{\\alpha t}+S_{\\alpha q}H_p=0$. Thus $\\dot\\beta=\\frac d{dt}S_\\alpha=S_{\\alpha t}+S_{\\alpha q}\\dot q=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "In the temporal chart, $x^0=ct$ implies $p_0=\\partial_{x^0}S=S_t/c$. The future mass-shell root [L2] says $p_0=-H/c$, hence $S_t+H(t,q,S_q)=0$. Conversely, substitution of this root into the covariant shell equation recovers the same time HJ equation, with the future sign fixed by $P^0>0$ and the nonzero null restriction when $m=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D4",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Here $p=S_q$ has units kg m/s, $q$ has metres, and $t$ seconds; the HJ equation makes $S_t$ energy, so $S$ has joule-seconds. The canonical generating identity therefore pairs length with momentum and respects the action units.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
