    "ex-rpm-h-two-shell-consistency-obstruction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-first-class-constraint-family",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(P,\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold with its induced Poisson bracket, and let $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ be a regular constraint family on a common zero surface $N=\\{z:\\chi_a(z)=0\\}$ as defined by [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. The family is **first-class on $N$** when\n\n$$\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}(z)=0\\qquad\\text{for every }z\\in N\\text{ and all }a,b.$$\n\nEquivalently, each pairwise bracket is weakly zero in the notation $F\\approx0$ of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. The Poisson bracket convention is that of [[thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow]]. This definition is a pointwise closure condition on the stated regular surface; it does not assert multiplier-algorithm termination or global quotient regularity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-constraint-consistency",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open interval and $(P,\\omega)$ a symplectic manifold. Let $\\chi_a:I\\times P\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth and suppose the covectors $d_z\\chi_a(\\lambda,z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in N_\\lambda:=\\{z:\\chi_1(\\lambda,z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(\\lambda,z)=0\\}$; this is the regular constraint condition of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]] on each slice. Let $H_c:I\\times P\\to\\mathbb R$ be a smooth ambient extension of a specified descended Hamiltonian. Work in a Darboux chart with coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$ and local potential $\\theta=p_i\\,dq^i$, so $d\\theta=-\\omega$ under the convention $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$; paths stay in this chart.\nFor any compact interval $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, paths $z:[a,b]\\to P$ that stay in the chart and smooth multipliers $u^a:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ have action\n\n$$I[z,u]=\\int_a^b\\left[\\theta(\\dot z)-H_c(\\lambda,z)-u^a(\\lambda)\\chi_a(\\lambda,z)\\right]d\\lambda.$$\n\nFixed-endpoint variations of $z$ and unrestricted variations of $u$ imply $z(\\lambda)\\in N_\\lambda$ and $\\dot z=X_{H_c(\\lambda,\\cdot)}+u^a(\\lambda)X_{\\chi_a(\\lambda,\\cdot)}$. A trajectory remains in the moving level set only when\n\n$$\\partial_\\lambda\\chi_a+\\{\\chi_a,H_c(\\lambda,\\cdot)\\}+u^b(\\lambda)\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}=0\\quad\\text{on }N_\\lambda.$$\n\nFor constraints independent of $\\lambda$, the first term is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The potential derivatives are $\\partial_{x_1^a}V=2V'(r^2)r_a$ and $\\partial_{x_2^a}V=-2V'(r^2)r_a$. The kinetic terms have $\\partial_{p_i^a}C_i=P_i^a$. In $\\{C_1,C_2\\}$, the first particle contributes $-P_1^a(2V'r_a)$ and the second contributes $(-2V'r_a)P_2^a$; all other cross terms vanish. Their sum is the displayed bracket.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose $r=(0,R,0,0)$ with $R>0$, $V(z)=\\lambda z$, and $\\lambda>0$. Let $P_i^1=u_i>0$, other spatial components zero, and $P_i^0=\\sqrt{m_i^2c^2+2\\lambda R^2+u_i^2}$. Then $p_i^2=-(P_i^0)^2+u_i^2=-m_i^2c^2-2\\lambda R^2$, so both $C_i=0$, while $r\\cdot(P_1+P_2)=R(u_1+u_2)>0$. Each constraint differential has nonzero momentum component $P_i$ in its own cotangent factor, so $dC_1,dC_2$ are independent at this witness. Independence persists on a sufficiently small neighborhood, making the local joint level regular. The bracket is $\\{C_1,C_2\\}=-2\\lambda R(u_1+u_2)\\ne0$ there, so [F1] says the pair is not first-class on that local regular surface. With autonomous constraints and $H_c=0$, [L1] gives $\\dot C_1=u^2\\{C_1,C_2\\}$ and $\\dot C_2=u^1\\{C_2,C_1\\}$; hence preserving both constraints at this point forces $u^1=u^2=0$. Thus arbitrary independent nonzero multipliers need not preserve the joint surface.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
