    "cex-rpm-h-relativistic-hj-caustic": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-local-hamilton-jacobi",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $H\\in C^\\infty(U,\\mathbb R)$ on an open set $U\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*\\mathbb R^n$, with coordinates $(t,q,p)$, and let $S_0\\in C^\\infty(Q_0,\\mathbb R)$ on an open $Q_0\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. For each $a_0\\in Q_0$ there are neighbourhoods of $(0,a_0)$ and a time interval on which the Hamilton characteristic system with $q(0,a)=a$, $p(0,a)=dS_0(a)$ has a smooth solution and $(t,a)\\mapsto(t,q(t,a))$ is a diffeomorphism onto its image after restriction. On that image the function\n\n$$S(t,q(t,a))=S_0(a)+\\int_0^t[p(u,a)\\cdot\\dot q(u,a)-H(u,q(u,a),p(u,a))],du$$\n\nis smooth, has initial value $S_0$, and satisfies $S_t+H(t,q,S_q)=0$. The claim is local to the invertible projection domain; it asserts no continuation after a caustic. $S$ has action units, so $[S]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[S_q]=[p]$, and $[S_t]=\\mathrm J$ when $t,q,p,H$ use SI time, length, momentum, and energy units.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-inertial-time-reduced-hamiltonian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In affine Minkowski space with $x^0=ct$, let $m\\ge0$, $p_i$ be spatial momentum components and $p_i\\ne0$ for $m=0$. On the future shell, $p_0=-E/c$, where\n\n$$E(\\mathbf p)=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2}.$$\n\nOn any compact interval $J=[t_1,t_2]$ of inertial time, the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order shell action to\n\n$$I_J=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\left(p_i\\dot q^i-E(\\mathbf p)\\right)dt,\\qquad q^i=x^i.$$\n\nThe gauge pair $C_m=0$, $\\psi=x^0-ct=0$ is second class because $\\{C_m,\\psi\\}=-P^0\\ne0$ on the future shell; its Dirac reduction gives the canonical spatial bracket. The reduced Hamilton equations are $\\dot q^i=c^2p_i/E$, $\\dot p_i=0$, agreeing with the unreduced characteristic trajectories. On this full flat future shell every characteristic line meets each inertial-time slice once; this global assertion does not extend by this proof to a curved temporal chart.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Hamilton's equations from [L2] give $\\dot p=0$, so $p(t,a)=S_0'(a)=-\\kappa a$. Also $\\dot q=E_p=c^2p/E$, hence integration with $q(0,a)=a$ gives the displayed formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate: $\\partial_aq=1-t\\kappa c^2\\frac d{dp}(p/E)$. Since $E^2=m^2c^4+c^2p^2$, $\\frac d{dp}(p/E)=m^2c^4/E^3$. Thus $\\partial_aq=1-t\\kappa m^2c^6/E^3$. Because $E\\ge mc^2$, this is at least $1-t\\kappa/m$, positive for $t<m/\\kappa$. At $a=0$, $p=0$ and $E=mc^2$, so it equals zero at $t=m/\\kappa$; this is the first singular point.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
